model {
  extend basalt.modelGateway {
    metadata {
      constraint "The observation credential is provisioned through its exact account-owned declaration only after a positive account token-write audit and expiring writer GO. A durable one-shot intent precedes the single admitted create request; an unknown reply never creates another credential. The actual infrastructure SDK retains the Redacted secret in native encrypted state for the direct Worker output, while provisioning journals retain only token identity and state hashes. Readback verifies the active token's exact account policy before provisioning completes. Ordinary releases cannot create, update or delete this credential. Bootstrap derives its one-resource native plan from the evaluated source declaration, preserves its downstream references, and resolves only the independently witnessed private namespace binding. That native apply cannot publish a Worker or apply unrelated changes. A dedicated main-only workflow shares the production release lock and existing encrypted State store. Its expiring GO covers the full operation, including the active-runtime read descriptor; run identity supplies the exclusive writer lease. Scope, candidate, account and source drift refuse before native-state access. Every application write joins fresh provider inventory to the adopted legacy boot and current selection through a passive private witness. Inspection remains read-only after the GO expires and exposes only native hashes and journal outcomes; it cannot settle or repeat an uncertain write."
    }
    metadata {
      constraint "Operator fleet writes require an expiring writer GO whose digest covers the exact command, complete fleet configuration, controller code, destination and bridge key digest. The signed transport receives a fresh envelope of at most sixty seconds at dispatch, while the reviewed action and payload remain fixed. The private bridge sends once and closes on every outcome. The operator retains a durable submission marker before sending; repeated execution can only inspect that same operation, even after its writer GO expires. A lost acknowledgment is recovered by inspecting the same retained operation, including after the writer lease expires, without reissuing a mutation. Fleet economics depend on removing each Home node's idle database work across warming, standby and draining states; more ready nodes alone are not evidence of lower total cost."
    }
    metadata {
      constraint "Fleet infrastructure is opt-in through two immutable same-account image pins. Blue and green have independent container namespaces, zero initial instances and provider maxima of four, while the single controller ledger limits all live and uncertain extra resources to four across both applications. The account-owned observation token has only container inventory read permission for that deployment account; its Redacted output goes directly to the Worker secret. An ordinary release still refuses unadmitted creation or an update of a fleet application. Missing pins cannot delete previously declared infrastructure through the guarded release path. No image pin or declared binding grants permission to start compute."
    }
    metadata {
      constraint "Namespace provisioning preserves every existing module byte when the reviewed bundle already exports the target class. Proven absence permits a placeholder; wildcard ambiguity, duplicate exports and malformed code refuse rather than overwrite existing code. The namespace-first provisioning path accepts only the original singleton or the exact blue and green classes. Each fleet application starts with zero instances and a provider maximum of four; class, binding, resource and journal identities must agree. An independent reviewed manifest and expiring writer GO are required per application, with exact legacy namespace and active boot retention. Unknown application creation remains observation-only. Provisioning alone neither enables fleet growth nor expands the shared four-resource ledger guard. Stage root and endpoint observations read their own production records independently across DO stores; their coordinator joins the snapshots. These reads cannot probe, start, drain or change the retained lifecycle."
    }
    metadata {
      constraint "During the first fleet transition, the retained legacy application uses its recorded immutable registry reference and original runtime input hash. A release still verifies the complete live provider baseline and image metadata before activation. Alchemy's reference-source hash stays separate from runtime provenance in the retained manifest. Changing the legacy image, configuration, namespace, placement or resource policy refuses preservation; a retained deletion policy alone is never a freeze. Fresh stages continue to build their own legacy image."
    }
    metadata {
      constraint "Fleet launch permission is a signed, revision-checked deployment command naming both private application bindings, their provider configuration and the retained legacy boot. Provider refusal prevents activation. The resource reservation checks the current permission in the same transaction as its four-resource guard. Disabling permission stops growth and drains existing resources; unknown outcomes remain inspectable and never dispatch another launch. Planned replacement reserves its new resource before starting and retains the old ready runtime until the replacement is positively ready. The root serializes reservations with its durable fence, then waits for already reserved calls to reach their send boundary before dispatching endpoint drain through the same retained DO stub. Failed or cancelled calls release that barrier without replay. The endpoint serializes model admission with drain before non-storage health waits, so neither delayed headers nor active streams hold up the drain acknowledgment. Both durable counts must reach zero before retirement. A missing endpoint drain acknowledgment keeps maintenance due even while the root still holds active streams. Ordinary authorized model traffic balances execution across the primary and fleet; management and shared account admission retain their original authority. Private peer interception is installed only after the exact launch witness is retained, and peer requests cannot start or warm compute. The controller resolves source and target through its owned live rows, while endpoint checks bind the actual launch or retained legacy boot. Opaque peer bytes retain Home mutual TLS. Fleet deadlines share the controller alarm without advancing Home collection. Primary lifecycle warmth records its Home deadline through the same scheduler, so an earlier fleet alarm cannot hide that deadline or consume later upkeep. Reconstruction preserves both deadlines and held primary request counts."
    }
    metadata {
      constraint "Runtime admission joins release-owned provider observations to a private trusted-start witness. A desired image binding or expected configuration hash is not evidence of the running image or its startup settings. A cold start acknowledgement never supplies the routing lease: after its single start send, the controller reads fresh health for that exact acknowledged boot. Only that health request consumes the lease window; delayed health, cancellation, identity drift and unknown start acknowledgements cannot authorize use or another start. The actual start callback retains the exact launch challenge and applied startup fingerprint before dispatch; authenticated readiness completes its boot. A failed witness write cannot start compute, and uncertain launch outcomes permit observation without a second start. Existing running compute may be preserved explicitly as unattested-active, never decoded as proven candidate evidence. Its adoption ACK and later passive readback derive the exact retained operation, manifest, decrypted capsule fingerprint, boot and committed selection from existing records. A fresh authenticated boot read and a selection recheck fence drift; capsule-only admission, another epoch, a candidate or draining slot, and an unknown ACK cannot authorize first-handoff advancement. Only a freshly joined attested candidate may pass promotion, with current controller, active selection and full positive and retired policy revisions still checked. The shared pool-runtime-attestation-v1 codec leaves existing slot and launch meanings unchanged. Private settings remain encrypted and stay out of receipts and logs. A manual main-only deployment credential audit reads token verification and self metadata without installing dependencies or making a provider write. Its output contains only status and permission-group names; unknown metadata does not establish token-creation permission. Ciphertext-corruption fixtures must change the decoded bytes and verify that precondition before expecting witness refusal; changing hex case alone is not corruption. Normal requests and explicit upkeep renew the selected runtime idle timer; synthetic probes and private health or witness reads never do. Legacy recovery cannot restart an admitted runtime, and initial adoption waits for any already admitted legacy restart to finish. Provider observations identify the exact named instance within a multi-instance application. Every active sibling must be running the same admitted image; an absent target, changed application settings or active rollout refuses admission. Instance-list ordering does not change observation identity. The new runtime class stays unbound until separately admitted provisioning and activation. A separate fleet ledger reserves before launch, counts warming and uncertain resources against the four-runtime guard, and retains held execution counts across reconstruction. Lost acknowledgements cannot dispatch a second launch or release capacity twice. Drain rejects delayed readiness; retired launch tombstones remain outside the bounded active-resource list. Logical cells isolate handoff journals while reading the existing canonical membership record, and cannot write that authority or start the legacy container. Scheduling state alone neither attests a runtime nor proves its endpoint has drained."
    }
  }
}
